# 🔥 7️⃣ Initialization Order With Inheritance

This is where the concept becomes really interesting.

Consider:

```java
class Parent {

    static {
        System.out.println("Parent Static Block");
    }

    {
        System.out.println("Parent Instance Block");
    }

    Parent() {
        System.out.println("Parent Constructor");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    static {
        System.out.println("Child Static Block");
    }

    {
        System.out.println("Child Instance Block");
    }

    Child() {
        System.out.println("Child Constructor");
    }
}
```

Now:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        new Child();
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
Parent Static Block
Child Static Block
Parent Instance Block
Parent Constructor
Child Instance Block
Child Constructor
```

---

# 🧩 Why Does This Happen?

Java must first prepare the parent before the child.

Conceptually:

```text
CLASS INITIALIZATION

Parent Static
        ↓
Child Static


OBJECT CREATION

Parent Instance
        ↓
Parent Constructor
        ↓
Child Instance
        ↓
Child Constructor
```

The parent part of the object must be initialized before the child part.

---

# 8️⃣ A Common Confusion — `super()`

Every constructor begins by calling a parent constructor.

If you don't explicitly write:

```java
super();
```

Java automatically inserts it when appropriate.

Example:

```java
class Child extends Parent {

    Child() {
        super();

        System.out.println("Child Constructor");
    }
}
```

This is why the parent constructor executes before the child constructor.

---

# 9️⃣ Why This Concept Matters

Understanding initialization order helps you:

* Debug unexpected output
* Understand object creation
* Work with inheritance
* Understand static initialization
* Avoid bugs caused by initialization dependencies
* Read framework code more easily

Frameworks such as dependency injection systems and application frameworks often depend heavily on understanding object and class lifecycles.

---

# ⚠️ Common Mistakes

### ❌ Mistake 1: Thinking Static Blocks Run for Every Object

```text
Wrong ❌

Every new object → Static block runs
```

Correct:

```text
Static block → Once when the class is initialized
```

---

### ❌ Mistake 2: Thinking Constructors Run Before Instance Blocks

Correct order:

```text
Instance Variables
        ↓
Instance Blocks
        ↓
Constructor
```

---

### ❌ Mistake 3: Ignoring Source Code Order

Within the same class:

```text
Java executes initialization in the order it appears.
```

This applies to:

* Static fields and static blocks
* Instance fields and instance blocks

---

